      "finite_checks": [],
      "risk_review": {
        "status": "complete",
        "reviewer": "alpha-batch-18",
        "notes": "Read Statement, F1–F8, Proof 1.1–4.1 and all eight current direct suppliers. For each fixed test pair, bounded class indicators are IID and L1; intersecting finitely many SLLN events gives all vote limits. A strict true-class gap gives eventual correctness, and a strict wrong-class gap gives eventual error even if wrong classes tie each other. J>=2 makes the wrong-class maximum nonempty; least-index voting resolves finite ties. Joint measurability makes the nonconvergence event measurable. Apply the finite product-law theorem to TWO random elements, the entire sequence and the test pair, not to infinitely many coordinates; both laws are probabilities. Fubini transfers the zero section probabilities to almost-every-sequence P-a.e. convergence, and DCT uses majorant 1. Zero margins are excluded explicitly, not silently assigned a vote limit. No choice or diagram is used. Breiman Theorem 1.2, printed p. 7 and Appendix I pp. 29–30 (PDF text lines 81–100 and 837–876), concerns the strict-margin formulation; its proof establishes vote-frequency convergence, not actual tie decisions at zero margin. The local proof supplies the extra null-margin hypothesis needed for actual conditional misclassification risk and does not rely on that source implication. No defect; supplier F1 integer-domain repair preserves its exact aggregation use."
      }
    },
