        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-moments-variance-and-covariance",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $X$ be a real random variable on a probability space.\n\n- For $p>0$, the **$p$th absolute moment** of $X$ exists when $|X|^p$ is\n  integrable, and is then\n  $$\\mathbb E[|X|^p].$$\n- If $X$ is integrable, its **mean** is $\\mathbb E[X]$.\n- If $X$ is square-integrable, its **variance** is\n  $$\\operatorname{Var}(X):=\\mathbb E[(X-\\mathbb E[X])^2].$$\n\nIf $X$ and $Y$ are square-integrable real random variables on the same\nprobability space, their **covariance** is\n$$\\operatorname{Cov}(X,Y):=\\mathbb E[(X-\\mathbb E[X])(Y-\\mathbb E[Y])].$$",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1",
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-independent-random-elements-have-product-joint-law",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $n\\ge1$, and let\n$X_i:(\\Omega,\\mathcal F,\\mathbb P)\\to(S_i,\\Sigma_i)$ for $i<n$ be independent\nrandom elements. Define\n\n$$X=(X_0,\\dots,X_{n-1}):\\Omega\\to\\prod_{i<n}S_i.$$\n\nThen $X$ is a random element of\n$\\left(\\prod_{i<n}S_i,\\bigotimes_{i<n}\\Sigma_i\\right)$, and its law is the\nfinite product of the marginal laws:\n\n$$\\mathbb P_X=\\bigotimes_{i<n}\\mathbb P_{X_i}.$$",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-fubini-theorem-for-l-one-on-sigma-finite-product-spaces",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X,\\mathcal A,\\mu)$ and $(Y,\\mathcal B,\\nu)$ be sigma-finite measure\nspaces, and let $f : X \\times Y \\to \\mathbb C$ belong to\n$L^1(\\mu \\times \\nu)$. Then:\n\n1. for $\\mu$-almost every $x$, the section $f_x$ belongs to $L^1(\\nu)$;\n2. for $\\nu$-almost every $y$, the section $f^y$ belongs to $L^1(\\mu)$;\n3. after assigning the value $0$ on the exceptional parameter sets, the\n   section-integral functions are integrable; and\n4. the three integrals agree:\n$$\\int_{X \\times Y} f\\,d(\\mu \\times \\nu) = \\int_X \\left(\\int_Y f_x\\,d\\nu\\right)d\\mu = \\int_Y \\left(\\int_X f^y\\,d\\mu\\right)d\\nu.$$",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1",
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "cor-cauchy-schwarz-for-random-variables",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "If $X,Y\\in L^2(\\mathbb P)$ are real random variables, then\n$$\\mathbb E[|XY|]\\le\\big(\\mathbb E[X^2]\\big)^{1/2}\\big(\\mathbb E[Y^2]\\big)^{1/2}.$$\n\nEquality holds if and only if at least one of $X,Y$ is zero almost surely, or\nthere is a constant $c>0$ with\n$$|X|^2=c\\,|Y|^2\\qquad\\mathbb P\\text{-almost surely}.$$",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "cor-chebyshev-inequality-for-random-variables",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "If $X$ is a square-integrable real random variable and $a>0$, then\n$$\\mathbb P(|X-\\mathbb E[X]|\\ge a)\\le\\frac{\\operatorname{Var}(X)}{a^2}.$$",
          "uses": [
            "4.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "raw-margin-conditional-mean",
          "claim": "For each fixed $z=(x,y)$, the definition of $\\hat j$ gives $\\mathbb E_Q[r_\\Theta(z)]=p_k(x)-p_{\\hat j(x,y)}(x)=m(z)$. Also $|r_\\theta(z)|\\le1$, so all displayed means and covariances are finite.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "margin-variance-covariance-identity",
          "claim": "The bounded jointly measurable class indicators and [F5] make $p_j$ and the finite maximizer $\\hat j$ measurable. By [F4] the pair $(\\Theta,\\Theta',Z)$ has product law, and [F5] permits exchanging the integrals of these bounded functions. Thus $\\mathbb E_{Q\\times Q}[\\operatorname{Cov}_P(r_\\Theta,r_{\\Theta'})]=\\mathbb E_P[m(Z)^2]-s^2=\\operatorname{Var}_P(m(Z))$. Indeed, the product of the two conditional raw-margin means is $m(z)^2$, while the product of their $P$-means integrates to $(\\mathbb E_Pm)^2=s^2$. If both standard deviations are positive, covariance equals correlation times their product; if either is zero, both the covariance and the weighted correlation term are zero. Thus the covariance expression for $\\rho$ is exactly Breiman's weighted mean raw-margin correlation.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "F5",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "raw-margin-variance-control",
          "claim": "Suppose $d>0$. From step 2.1, $\\rho=\\operatorname{Var}_P(m)/d^2\\ge0$. Since $r_\\theta^2\\le1$, $\\mathbb E_Q[sd(\\Theta)^2]=\\mathbb E_{Q\\times P}[r_\\Theta^2]-\\mathbb E_Q[(\\mathbb E_Pr_\\Theta)^2]\\le1-s^2$. The final inequality uses $\\mathbb E_Q[(\\mathbb E_Pr_\\Theta)^2]\\ge(\\mathbb E_Q\\mathbb E_Pr_\\Theta)^2=s^2$, by [F6] with one factor equal to $1$; [F5] and step 1.1 give $\\mathbb E_Q\\mathbb E_Pr_\\Theta=s$. The same inequality gives $d^2\\le\\mathbb E_Q[sd(\\Theta)^2]$. Therefore $\\operatorname{Var}_P(m)=\\rho d^2\\le\\rho(1-s^2)$.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F5",
            "F6",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "chebyshev-error-bound",
          "claim": "Since $s>0$, the event $\\{m(Z)<0\\}$ is contained in $\\{|m(Z)-s|\\ge s\\}$. Applying [F7] and then step 3.1 gives $P\\{m(Z)<0\\}\\le\\operatorname{Var}_P(m(Z))/s^2\\le\\rho(1-s^2)/s^2$.",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F7",
            "step 3.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "zero-denominator-case",
          "claim": "If $d=0$, then $sd\\ge0$ and $\\mathbb E_Qsd=0$. For each $n\\ge1$, $Q\\{sd\\ge1/n\\}=0$, since otherwise $\\mathbb E_Qsd>0$; taking the countable union gives $sd=0$ $Q$-almost everywhere. For each such $\\theta$, $\\mathbb E_P(r_\\theta-\\mathbb E_Pr_\\theta)^2=0$, so the same nonnegative-integral argument makes $r_\\theta(Z)=\\mathbb E_Pr_\\theta$ $P$-almost surely. By [F5], $m(Z)=\\mathbb E_Qr_\\Theta(Z)$ is therefore the constant $\\mathbb E_Pm(Z)=s>0$ for $P$-almost every $Z$. Hence $P\\{m(Z)<0\\}=0$, as claimed in this case.",
          "step": "5.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F5",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
