      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "centered-residual-covariance",
          "claim": "For every allowed $\\theta$, the mean condition gives $\\mathbb E_Pe_\\theta(Z)=0$. Hence [F3] gives $\\operatorname{Cov}_P(e_\\theta,e_{\\theta'})=\\mathbb E_P[e_\\theta e_{\\theta'}]$. If both residual standard deviations are positive, define their correlation as this covariance divided by their product; if either is zero, the covariance is zero. Thus the covariance expression for $\\rho$ agrees with Breiman's weighted mean residual correlation, including zero-standard-deviation pairs. The products are integrable by [F6] and square-integrability. Also $\\mathbb E_{P\\times Q}e_\\Theta^2<\\infty$, since $(a-b)^2\\le2a^2+2b^2$ and both $Y$ and $h(X,\\Theta)$ are square-integrable.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F6",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "forest-risk-covariance-identity",
          "claim": "By [F4] the pair $(\\Theta,\\Theta',Z)$ has product law. For $P$-almost every $z$, [F6] gives $(\\mathbb E_Q|e_\\Theta(z)|)^2\\le\\mathbb E_Qe_\\Theta(z)^2$; integrating and using step 1.1 shows $e_\\theta(z)e_{\\theta'}(z)$ is integrable on $P\\times Q\\times Q$. Thus [F5] permits the following integrations. The $Q$-average is the infinite-forest prediction obtained from the same regression tree predictions that the algorithm averages at finite $B$ [F1]. Also $\\mathbb E_Qe_\\Theta(Z)=Y-\\mathbb E_Qh(X,\\Theta)$ for $P$-almost every $Z$. Consequently $PE^*(\\mathrm{forest})=\\mathbb E_P[(\\mathbb E_Qe_\\Theta(Z))^2]=\\mathbb E_{Q\\times Q}[\\mathbb E_P(e_\\Theta(Z)e_{\\Theta'}(Z))]=\\mathbb E_{Q\\times Q}[\\operatorname{Cov}_P(e_\\Theta(Z),e_{\\Theta'}(Z))]$. Thus when $d>0$, $PE^*(\\mathrm{forest})=\\rho d^2$ and $\\rho\\ge0$.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F4",
            "F5",
            "F6",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "tree-risk-and-correlation-bound",
          "claim": "The zero-mean condition and [F3] give $PE^*(\\mathrm{tree})=\\mathbb E_Q[sd(\\Theta)^2]$. By [F6] with one factor equal to $1$, $d^2=(\\mathbb E_Qsd(\\Theta))^2\\le\\mathbb E_Q[sd(\\Theta)^2]$. For $d>0$, combining with step 2.1 and $\\rho\\ge0$ yields $PE^*(\\mathrm{forest})\\le\\rho\\,PE^*(\\mathrm{tree})$.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F6",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "zero-denominator-risks",
          "claim": "If $d=0$, then the nonnegative function $sd$ vanishes $Q$-almost everywhere: each set $\\{sd\\ge1/n\\}$ has measure zero since otherwise $\\mathbb E_Qsd>0$. For $Q$-almost every $\\theta$, the mean-zero residual has variance zero, so the same argument applied to $(e_\\theta-\\mathbb E_Pe_\\theta)^2$ gives $e_\\theta=0$ $P$-almost surely. Fubini then gives $e_\\Theta(Z)=0$ for $P\\times Q$-almost every $(Z,\\Theta)$; both displayed risks are zero.",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F5",
            "step 1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
