        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-empirical-decision-tree",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Fix a finite labeled sample $(x_i,y_i)_{i=1}^n$ with $n\\ge1$, $x_i\\in\\mathbb R^p$ and $p\\ge1$. In regression, $y_i\\in\\mathbb R$. In classification, the labels lie in a fixed ordered finite set $\\mathcal C=\\{c_1<\\cdots<c_K\\}$ with $K\\ge1$. A finite empirical axis-aligned binary decision tree has root sample-index set $I_{\\mathrm{root}}=\\{1,\\ldots,n\\}$. Each internal node $v$ stores a coordinate $j\\in\\{1,\\ldots,p\\}$ and threshold $t\\in\\mathbb R$; its left and right child sample sets are $I_v^L=\\{i\\in I_v:x_{ij}\\le t\\}$ and $I_v^R=\\{i\\in I_v:x_{ij}>t\\}$, and both are required to be nonempty. A leaf is a terminal node. The leaf sample sets partition $\\{1,\\ldots,n\\}$, and each node's feature region is the intersection of its path inequalities. Every input feature vector follows the unique path determined by the stored comparisons.\n\nFor a regression leaf with sample set $I$, put $N_I=|I|$, $\\bar y_I=N_I^{-1}\\sum_{i\\in I}y_i$, and $Q_I=N_I^{-1}\\sum_{i\\in I}(y_i-\\bar y_I)^2$; its prediction is $\\bar y_I$ and its total squared-error loss is $N_IQ_I$. For a classification leaf, put $p_{Ik}=N_I^{-1}|\\{i\\in I:y_i=c_k\\}|$ and predict the class $c_k$ with largest $p_{Ik}$, choosing the least index in a tie. Its optional impurities are misclassification $Q_I^{\\mathrm{mis}}=1-\\max_k p_{Ik}$, Gini $Q_I^{\\mathrm{Gini}}=1-\\sum_{k=1}^Kp_{Ik}^2$, and entropy $Q_I^{\\mathrm{ent}}=-\\sum_{k=1}^Kp_{Ik}\\log p_{Ik}$, where $\\log$ is the natural logarithm and $0\\log0=0$. A chosen classification criterion determines $Q_I$ and the corresponding total leaf impurity is $N_IQ_I$.",
